<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Visigothic Code</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/654">654</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Liber Iudiciorum, Forum Iudicum, Fuero Juzgo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Visigothic Code, or Liber Iudiciorum, is the law code of the Visigothic kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, promulgated by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recceswinth">Recceswinth</a> in 654 and revised by Erwig in 681. Unlike earlier codes it applied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and Hispano-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> alike. It remained in use in the Christian kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> and León and among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozarabs">Mozarabs</a>, and was translated into Castilian in the thirteenth century as the Fuero Juzgo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/visigothic-code.jpg" alt="Visigothic Code" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LiberIudiciorum01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the first centuries of Visigothic rule, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> lived under different laws. The earliest known Visigothic laws, the Code of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euric">Euric</a>, were compiled by about 480, and the Breviary of Alaric, based on Roman imperial law and meant for the Roman majority, was promulgated at a gathering of nobles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> on 2 February 506. King Leovigild attempted a revised code for both peoples, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> at the Third Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 589, King Reccared issued laws that applied equally to both.</p><p>The code itself was begun by King Chindasuinth in the second year of his reign, 642–643, but his text had been hastily written, and the version his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recceswinth">Recceswinth</a> published was edited by Braulio of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. Later kings, Wamba, Erwig, Egica and perhaps Wittiza, added laws of their own; Erwig's revision at the Twelfth Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 681 included 28 new laws dealing with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The code is arranged in twelve books, from the lawmaker and the conduct of trials through marriage, inheritance, contracts, crimes, theft, violence, fugitives and boundaries to physicians, the dead and merchants from overseas. It fused Roman and Germanic law, abolished the separate laws for romani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothi">gothi</a> so that all subjects became hispani, and absorbed much of the Catholic Church's canon law, which gives it a strongly theocratic tone. Women could inherit land and titles, manage property apart from their husbands, make wills if they had no heirs, testify in court from the age of 14 and arrange their own marriages from 20. Its last book is directed against heretics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The laws stayed in force long after the kingdom fell. Monastic charters in 10th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> refer to them, and under Muslim rule Christians were allowed their own law in return for paying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>, so the code probably remained the authority of Christian magistrates. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> he ordered it observed and translated, inaccurately, as the Fuero Juzgo. Its Occitan translation, the Llibre Jutge of about 1050, is among the oldest texts in that language. Samuel Parsons Scott's English translation of 1910 was severely criticised.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Visigothic Code” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Visigothic law code, the <b>Liber Iudiciorum</b>, continued to be cited in legal disputes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Liber Iudiciorum</b>, the Visigothic law code, remained the basis of law, supplemented by local charters and custom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Liber Iudiciorum</b>, promulgated by Chindaswinth and completed by Recceswinth in 654, replaced all older codes and the separate laws of Goths and Hispano-Romans with one law based almost wholly on Roman law; women could inherit land and title, dispose of property by will, bear witness in court…</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
